  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 201 PM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nSynoptically, not much has changed for the Long Term period. Sunday \nthe ridge will continue to de-amplify as a trough begins to move \nthrough Canada/US border. Come Monday, we begin to transition to \nmore zonal flow. Once again throughout the week, there looks to be \nsome shortwaves moving through the area that may create some \nprecipitation chances for the County Warning Area (CWA).\n\nOverall for the long term, high temperatures are forecast to be in \nthe high 90s for the majority of the CWA. However Wednesday we have \na brief \"cool\" down where highs should be in the low to mid 90s. \nWinds for the week look to be breezy for the majority of the CWA. \nStarting on Monday, the CWA's precipitation chances begin to \nincrease. As each shortwave moves through, there is around a 20-40% \nchance for showers Monday through Wednesday. Thursday and Friday \nchances begin to decrease to 10-20%.\n\nFor this period there are some localized elevated fire weather \nconcerns for Sunday and Monday during the afternoon hours. Beginning \nwith Sunday, wind gusts are forecast to be in the 20-30 mph range \nand mainly from the south. Relative humidity (RH) values look to be \nin the upper teens to low 20%s. The lowest RH values look to be \nmainly west of KS Hwy 27. Moving to GFDI, the maximum values are \nforecast to be in the 30-45 range mainly for the northwest portion \nof the CWA. These values would promote very high fire growth. With \nthis being said, there are some concerns with whether or not all of \nthese conditions will occur at the same time. \n\nMonday looks very similar to Sunday with the changes of high \ntemperatures in the low 100s for the majority of the CWA. RH values \nare forecast to be in the mid teens to low 20%s for the majority of \nthe CWA. The lowest values again are for areas west of KS Hwy 27. \nGusts are once again mainly from the south and in the range of 20-30 \nmph. GFDI values are forecast to be in the 30-59 for the majority of \nthe CWA. One other thing to mention is Monday does have a chance of \nseeing thunderstorms, so dry lightning may also pose a risk wherever \nstorms develop.",
